## Tools and environment
- Device: Android, id from `adb devices` (set as `DEVICE_UDID` for the helper), traffic via HTTP Toolkit proxy (port 8000).
- HTTP Toolkit MCP is READ-ONLY: list events, read bodies. Mock/Modify rules must be created manually in the UI. To force a 4xx/5xx the rule must set the HTTP status. Event list resets per session/day: fetch the newest matching event.
- Screenshots: `adb shell screencap -p /sdcard/_sc.png` -> `adb pull /sdcard/_sc.png ".\<name>.png"` -> `adb shell rm /sdcard/_sc.png`. Never use PowerShell `>` (corrupts PNG). Long screens: `adb shell input swipe <x> <y1> <y2> 1000`, save `_p1.png`, `_p2.png`, ...
- Appium: `npm run appium:start` (port 4723). UiAutomator2 driver 5.0.3, Android 15 caps.
- Flutter: no usable `text`/`resource-id`. Use accessibility `content-desc` selectors with partial (`contains`) matching. Counts are embedded in labels (e.g. `Draft claims\n2`).
- Shell: PowerShell 5.1. Chain with `;`, not `&&`. For non-trivial JS, write a temp `.js` file, run it, delete it.
- MCP servers: HTTP Toolkit, Azure DevOps (wiki, work items, test plans, pipelines, repos), Playwright (web), Azure (azmcp, rare), Context7 (library docs).

## App behaviour
- Auth: bearer token, ~30 min expiry. Idle sessions fall back to login; re-login before capturing.
- Forms are provider-driven: the app does not own field structure or validation.
- Behaviour gated by backend flags: canEdit, canDelete, canUnlinkExpenses, canLinkExpenses, supportsExpenseLinking, processingType.

## Conventions
- Start every flow from a fresh screen. Reused forms leave stale values that look like bugs.
- Do not create markdown files documenting changes unless asked. Edit the relevant file directly.
- Re-read a file before editing: the user may hand-edit it.
- Filenames often have no extension. Match the convention for sibling docs.
- Put reference docs and manual-test evidence in the feature folder. Scripts go in `tools/`, generated output in `tools/_out/`.

## Task checklist
1. Identify domain (Expenses / Claims / Approvals) and perspective (requester / approver).
2. Pull behaviour from the wiki; cross-check `Claims/Claim_APIrequests.json`.
3. For live checks: device logged in, traffic via HTTP Toolkit, evidence via adb pipeline.
4. For mocks: user creates the Modify rule in HTTP Toolkit, then re-scan from a fresh form.
5. Record findings in the existing feature doc. Raise bugs/test cases in ADO only when asked.
